WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Hi. It's Mr. Andersen and in this podcast I'm going to talk about the skeletal

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.000
system. Before we get to the human skeletal system you should know that there are two

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.000
different types of skeleton. We could have an exoskeleton. In other words we could have

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:21.000
our skeleton on the outside, like we find in these beetles. Or we can have an endoskeleton.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.000
That's like us. We have our skeleton on the inside. One great thing about an endoskeleton

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.000
is it can actually support greater weight. That's why we can get very big and we're never

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.000
going to have insects the size of an elephant. And one of the bad things about having an

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.000
exoskeleton is that as you grow you're going to quickly outgrow your skeleton. So you'll

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.000
have to molt. I don't want to say either of these is better because insects rule the world.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.000
In fact beetles rule the world. But know that there are two different types. If we're talking

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.000
about endoskeletons though it's built on a bone. And if we were to think of, when you

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.000
think of a bone, you generally think of a bone, like a dried out piece of wood almost.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.000
And nothing could be farther from the truth. And so a bone is live. It's living material.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.000
It's filled up with all of these capillaries. And it's exchanging nutrients with the blood

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.000
system. And so this is a better picture of what a bone is like. This is actually the

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.000
ball on the top of a femur that was cut off. And so around the outside of a bone you have

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.000
what's called compact bone. And then as we move to inside, you're going to have spongy

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:26.000
bone. And then on the inside you're going to have marrow. And that marrow can be one

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.000
of two types. It could be red marrow or yellow marrow. Red marrow, just because of its color,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.000
red marrow is mostly going to have the building blocks of blood. And that's one of the important

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
roles of marrow. And then the yellow marrow is going to have way more of this fat. Storing

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:46.000
these fatty acids for a time when we actually need it. And so this is a more intense picture

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.000
of bone, excuse me, or a diagram of that. And so you can see the spongy bone on the

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:55.000
inside. We've got this compact bone on the outside. But you can see these capillaries

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.000
are feeding the bone with nutrients. Okay. What's the function of the skeletal system?

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.000
We can look at them over here. It's going to be support, movements, storage, blood and

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.000
then homeostasis. So let's start with the thing that we're all familiar with. Function

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:14.000
of our skeleton is to support us. It holds me up. It also offers protection. And so I

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.000
wouldn't be able to stand upright if it weren't for my endoskeleton. And also it protects

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.000
vital organs. In other words the skull is protecting my brain. Or this ribcage is protecting

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.000
my organs in my thoracic cavity. Our skeleton is broken down into two different parts. We

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.000
have the axial skeleton. Axial skeleton is going to be made up of the skull, the vertebral

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.000
column and the ribs. That is really protecting the central nervous system. And a lot of these

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.000
organs. And then we have the appendicular skeleton. Appendicular skeleton is made up

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.000
of everything pretty much out past these joints. And so that's going to be you know your hip,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.000
your shoulder blade and your arms and legs. The appendicular skeleton, the function of

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.000
that is in locomotion. So speaking of that let's get to the next one which is movement.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Bones allow us to move. But they can't move without muscles. And so one of the big things

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.000
required for skeletal system are going to be skeletal muscles. They're not directly

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:12.000
attached to the bone. And so they're going to be attached from muscle to bone with a

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.000
tendon. And that's going to be attached to the bone itself. Now bone can also be attached

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:21.000
to bone using what is called a ligament. But the function of it is to give us movement.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:27.000
In order to get movement, you have to, since bone is static, you have to have a joint between

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.000
adjoining bones so you can actually get movement. And there are five general types of sockets

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.000
that you can have. And they each give us different movement. They are ball and socket, condyloid,

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:42.000
saddle, hinge and pivot joint. Let's quickly go through those. If it's a ball and socket

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.000
joint, it's basically a ball fitting inside a socket. And so it gives us a huge amount

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:52.000
of maneuverability. Two examples in us would be the hip. So this would be the hip joint.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:58.000
And then the shoulder joint. What's great about these is I have a huge amount maneuverability

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.000
in my shoulder joint. What's bad about that? Think of all the ligaments that are holding

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:07.000
that in place. And so there's a lot of movement but there can also be a lot of things that

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.000
go wrong with a ball and socket joint. And that's why, especially hip replacement is

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.000
very common. Next we get to the condyloid. If you look at a condyloid we essentially

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.000
have this pedestal like this but then we have this going that sits on the top of it. A perfect

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.000
example of that would be the wrist bone. And so what that wrist bone gives me is it's going

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:31.000
to give me ability to do this flexion, extension and then I'm also going to be adduct and abduct,

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:36.000
be able to go either way like that. But I don't have all of the maneuverability that

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:41.000
I would get with a ball and socket joint. Next we go to the saddle joint. So saddle joint

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:46.000
essentially looks like this. Great example of that would be the thumb. So the thumb is

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.000
sitting on one kind of bone sitting on another. And I get a huge amount of maneuverability

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:56.000
in my thumb that I just don't have in my fingers. And so we have an opposable thumb. That basically

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.000
means you can touch all of the other fingers with your thumb. And that's not super common

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.000
in the animal world. But you can thank your saddle joint for that. Next we've got the

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.000
hinge joint. Just like a door hinge that only moves in one direction. And so your fingers

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.000
or your phalanges have that. So basically when you go like this, that's a hinge joint.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:19.000
And you can't move, hopefully you can't move your finger in either direction using a hinge

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.000
joint. And then the last one we have is a pivot joint. A great example of that would

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.000
be where the humerus meets the radius and the ulna. And so my, right here, it's going

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.000
to be right here at my elbow, basically what I can do is I can move my arm this way and

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.000
I can move it like that. And so it's just pivoting around that center point. So all

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.000
these are joints and they allow movement inside the bones. Now the bones do other things as

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:45.000
well. They're going to be a site of storage. And so it's a living organ. And so if you

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:50.000
look inside here, you can see how alive it is. This is actually a diagram of it. What

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.000
you'll have are these Haversian canals that go through the middle. And that allows blood

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:58.000
to flow through it. But then you're going to have these osteocytes which are these cells.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:02.000
This is a zoomed in version of it. They almost look like a star. And those are laying down

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:08.000
new amounts of blood or excuse me, bone. And that bone is made up of chemicals. So it's

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:14.000
a lot of calcium, phosphorus. A number of other chemicals. But basically it's sitting

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.000
there. It's giving its structure but it's also storing it. And so when we need calcium

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:23.000
or we need phosphorus or when we need fatty acids, when we need fat, then we can grab

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:28.000
that from our bone. Because it's connected right to the circulatory system. It's also

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.000
super important in blood. And so if we were looking in the marrow, we have what are called

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:38.000
pluripotent or excuse me, multi potent stem cells. So we have these cells that could become

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:43.000
any other cell. In fact, look at all the cells that they can become. They can be erythrocytes

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:47.000
which are going to be like red blood cells. But they can also be macrophages and lymphocytes

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:53.000
as well. That was the bell. So I've got to hurry up. Last thing that they do is they

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:58.000
help us maintain homeostasis. And so they do that by regulating the amount of blood

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:03.000
calcium we have. So a quick example of that. We have a thyroid and a parathyroid gland.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:08.000
So basically if our blood calcium level goes too high, then our thyroid gland is going

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:15.000
to secrete a chemical called calcitonin. And that's going to lower the blood, the blood

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:19.000
calcium level. So it's going to do that by taking in calcium into the bone. That's going

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:24.000
to drop our levels. We're also going to secrete calcium in the kidneys. Likewise, if it goes

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:29.000
too low, our parathyroid hormone is going to be secreted by the parathyroid. What's

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:33.000
that going to do in the bone? The bone, since it's storing all this calcium, it can now

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.000
release that into the blood. We'll hold onto it in the kidney and we'll also get it in

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:42.000
the intestines. But all of these things are going to allows us to maintain a stable internal

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:48.000
environment. The nice thing about the bone is they're alive and they're connected right

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.000
to the circulatory system. So if we need something we can get it really quickly. And so that's

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:56.000
the skeletal system. It holds it up. It holds us up but it does a lot more than that as

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:58.000
well. And so I hope that's helpful.
